    "title": "Strike averted: LAUSD schools will open Tuesday after SEIU Local 99 deal",
    "text": "Los Angeles Unified School District campuses will be open Tuesday after a strike involving essential workers was averted, officials on both sides confirmed. Service Employees International Union Local 99, which represents about 30,000 essential LAUSD workers, announced a tentative deal with the district early Tuesday morning. The agreement includes a 24% wage increase, increased work hours to ensure health care benefits for thousands of workers, the rescinding of layoffs for hundreds of IT technicians, and expanded health care coverage for teacher assistants, after-school workers, and others. LAUSD confirmed the agreement on its website Tuesday morning. “We are pleased to announce that we have reached an agreement in principle with SEIU Local 99 that will allow schools to be open,” the district said. SEIU Local 99 was the third and final union deal the district needed to reach to avert a strike that would have effectively shut down schools Tuesday. “We are proud to have reached resolution with all of our labor partners, UTLA, SEIU, and AALA Teamsters Local 2010, ensuring stability for our schools and continuity for the students and families we serve,” Acting Superintendent Andrés E. Chait said. While the deals still need to be ratified by union members, schools will remain open in the meantime. Los Angeles Mayor Karen Bass also stepped in to help push negotiations forward and work on contingency plans in case of a strike. “Our schools’ teachers, instructional aides, nurses, custodians, bus drivers, and other dedicated workers care for our children every day, and they deserve a fair contract,” Bass’ office said in a statement. “Now, there is one on the table, and class is in session tomorrow.”",
